cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "EVAR Planner — an open-source automated endovascular aneurysm-repair planner"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata. Research use only — NOT a regulated medical device."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Stonko
    given-names: "David P."
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2804-3857"
repository-code: "https://github.com/davidstonko/aortic-segmenter-and-surgery-planner"
url: "https://localminimum.us/research/"
abstract: >-
  EVAR Planner is an open-source automated endovascular aneurysm-repair
  (EVAR) planner that starts from a raw arterial-phase CT angiogram. The
  headless pipeline ingests DICOM, segments the aorta, iliacs, kidneys
  and liver with TotalSegmentator (or a learned or externally supplied
  label map), detects the renal, celiac and superior mesenteric
  branches, extends the iliacs into the common femoral arteries and
  repairs connectivity, places anatomic auto-seeds without user clicks,
  builds a bifurcated centerline (VMTK Voronoi/fast-marching when
  installed, a pure-MATLAB skeleton-graph fallback otherwise), derives
  contrast-lumen sizing measurements (proximal neck diameter, length
  from the lowest renal ostium, and angulation; iliac diameters; peak
  aneurysm diameter), and matches them against the instructions-for-use
  criteria of seven catalogued stent grafts. No device is recommended
  when quality control fails. Each segmentation is checked by a
  six-block completeness audit (required vessels, visceral branches,
  vessel sizes, proximal extent, per-side continuity, centerline
  curvature), and the iliac-to-femoral tracking is checked by twelve
  SE(3) anatomic-plausibility rules (five per-centerline, seven
  cross-vessel) whose thresholds were calibrated against the 99th
  percentile of the AAA-100 reference cohort (Zenodo 10932957). A
  MATLAB GUI exposes the same pipeline with operator review at each
  step. Measurements have not yet been validated against a reference
  workstation. Research use only; not a medical device.
keywords:
  - EVAR
  - TEVAR
  - endovascular
  - aortic aneurysm
  - vascular surgery
  - centerline extraction
  - stent graft sizing
  - medical image analysis
  - MATLAB
  - open source
